[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting on router1, specifically the OSPF routing daemon (ospfd) and the FRR service supervision. The FRR service is failed and only zebra is running; the OSPF process is not.

Mechanism: With OSPF down on router1, it no longer forms adjacencies with neighbors or learns/advertises inter-zone routes. As a result, only directly connected networks and a couple of pre-existing kernel static routes remain in the FIB. That preserves intra-zone reachability (hosts can reach their local gateway) and some paths covered by leftover kernel routes, but inter-zone paths that relied on dynamic OSPF distribution disappear—manifesting as partial cross-zone reachability.

Localization and evidence:
- systemctl on router1: “frr.service … Active: failed (failed).”
- Process list on router1 shows only zebra; no ospfd/bgpd.
- vtysh: “ospfd is not running.”
- Routing table shows only C (connected) and K (kernel) routes; no OSPF (O) routes: e.g., C>* 10.16.1.0/24 (eth2), C>* 10.20.1.0/24 (eth3), K>* 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 via 10.20.1.2.
- /etc/frr/frr.conf is missing (“No such file or directory”) and /etc/frr/daemons has ospfd=no.

Root cause: A recent change on router1 disabled/removed OSPF (ospfd=no and frr.conf missing), causing the FRR service to fail and routing to stop.

Corrective fix: Restore a valid /etc/frr/frr.conf with the OSPF configuration, set ospfd=yes in /etc/frr/daemons, then systemctl restart frr. Verify adjacencies with “show ip ospf neighbor” and confirm OSPF routes reappear in the table.
=== END ===